s no clear change: +0.04\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.15 to +0.22) that includes zero. Los Olivos' average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.70\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.52 to +0.88), about +4.9\u00b0F since 1955. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1980, lows have fallen <strong class='sig'>\u22120.36\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range \u22120.70 to \u22120.01) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.70\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.32 to +1.08). At a volunteer station, a change in the exact site, the instruments or the time of day it's read can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so read both periods with care.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 5 of 12 months. Highs rose in 11 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Los Olivos' yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.51 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 21.0 inches a year. The wettest year was 1995 (43.80 in); the driest, 2015 (4.78 in). Los Olivos almost never sees snow (the 1991\u20132020 average is 0.0 inches a winter), so there's no snowfall chart.","show_snow":false,"sig_bars":{"lo":false,"hi":true},"t_year":"2026 so far: 13 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}